# F04's live-match half: during a real match, does the engine's own pad hold a control -- and hold
# one *while* the main stick is moved -- and does the game answer a control whose mapping can be
# observed in its own state?
#
# What this is, and what it is not. The route is r2-audio's, verbatim, so the match is reached the
# same way the audio row reaches it: the front end's own screens, driven by the port's
# scene-anchored presses, with no guest schedule, no fake frames and no skipped scene. What is
# injected is the sweep itself, on the port's control channel (STRIKERS_CONTROL, press/stick), and
# that is a *control-channel injection*, not a UI touch. XCUIAutomation has no multi-touch, so two
# held real touches cannot be produced, and this scenario does not pretend otherwise: the runner's
# row says which half is a touch (S.f04.ui-touch-sweep, every one of the twelve controls drawn by a
# real finger and read back off the engine's own pad) and which half is injected (this one).
#
# Where the measurement is taken, and why an injection is admissible there. src/Game/main.cpp lays
# a frame out as PortUpdateSyntheticInput -- the host's offer, then this channel's held button and
# stick, merged by Aurora's own dominant_axis rule rather than assigned over the offer -- then
# PortHostUIFrame, then PortInvokePadSamplingCallback, which runs the registered VBlank pad pass
# that clamps what it read and swaps it into PadStatus::s_Current[0]. A finger on the glass and a
# line in the control file therefore reach the same merge and are clamped by the same pass, which is
# why doc 34's "inputs must reach the same sampling boundary as human controls" holds for the
# channel: the pad this row reads is the pad the game's tasks read.
#
# The stick is held at 25 rather than 100 on purpose. The port's left-analog-to-d-pad map
# (src/NL/plat/platpad.cpp) ORs a compass sector into the sample only once the clamped stick reaches
# 0.6 of its 56 radius -- 33.6 -- so a hold of 100 would put a RIGHT bit in the mask the host never
# pressed and make "which bit came from the press" ambiguous. 25 of 100 is 31 raw, which the game's
# own clamp takes past its 15 deadzone to 16: nonzero, past the deadzone, below the map's threshold.
# Every bit in the mask during the sweep is therefore a bit a press made.
#
# Start is the last press inside the bracket because its mapping is observable as the game's own
# response rather than as a bit: FrontEnd::UpdateForGame turns a Start press into EnterMenuState(
# MET_PAUSE) and m_bInPauseMenuState, which the port's dump prints as pause=1. The two dumps after
# it exist because the pause is read by PortDebugFrame at the top of a frame and the press is
# executed later in the same frame, so the dump that follows the press by one frame can still have
# been filled before the game answered it; three consecutive dumps bound that lag without inventing
# a sleep. A control whose mapping can be watched in the game's own state is the stronger half of
# doc 34's "verify game response, not merely button hittability".
#
# health -> saving/loading -> legal -> intro movie -> title -> main menu -> mariobg -> choose
# captains (both sides) -> stadium select -> loading -> live match -> the sweep -> quit.
env STRIKERS_LOG_SCENES=1
env STRIKERS_LOG_MATCH=1
env STRIKERS_LOG_CONSUME=1
env STRIKERS_AUTOPRESS=A@s51+180,A@s2+240,A@s53+120,A@s3+240,A@s8+300,A@s8+420,A@s8+540,A@s8+660,LEFT@s8+780,A@s8+900,A@s8+1020,A@s9+300
require-scene choose_captains
wait-log state=4 ot=0 score @420
dump f04_live_start
stick 25 0 900
press A 6
press B 6
press X 6
press Y 6
press Z 6
press L 6
press R 6
press UP 6
press DOWN 6
press LEFT 6
press RIGHT 6
press START 6
dump f04_live_end
dump f04_pause_1
dump f04_pause_2
quit
